  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1125 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nSHOWERS TUESDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY MORNING:\n\nDry conditions with increasing clouds are expected today. The next \nsystem moves across the area Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday \nmorning. Strong southwesterly winds develop Tuesday ahead of the \nsystem. Gusts tomorrow could reach 45 mph especially across the \nsouthwest half of the area. Showers and perhaps a few embedded \nthunderstorms develop Tuesday afternoon and continue through \nWednesday morning. Rainfall amounts in the valleys are generally \nunder 0.5\" with most places seeing a trace-0.25\". Snow levels will \nstart around 9-10kft when showers arrive and drop to around 6-7kft \novernight. The southern mountains and Utah mountains will be favored \nfor this event. Snowfall amounts above 9kft will range from 4-10\" \nwith lesser amounts for lower elevations. The other mountain ranges \ncould expect more like 1-4\". Blowing snow and snow packed roads \nmay cause travel impacts on the mountain passes Tuesday night. \n\nShowers will taper off around daybreak Wednesday, as this \nsystem exits the region. A few lingering snow showers will \ncontinue along the Divide Wednesday morning. Cooler and drier \nconditions develop on Wednesday with temperatures 10 to 15 \ndegrees colder behind the front. A weak transitory ridge moves \noverhead Wednesday and Thursday as another more organized system\ncomes ashore out west.\n\nNEXT SYSTEM FRIDAY AND SATURDAY: \n\nForecast models continue to show another system arriving late \nThursday night through Saturday morning. Models still show an open \nwave setup. Precipitation totals continue to vary by model run, with \nthe central and northern mountains of Colorado picking up additional \nsnow in the latest runs. Differences among the models with regards \nto strength and timing keep confidence low attm. Most of the \nmoisture is expected to arrive ahead of the cold front, making \ntiming critical. Snow levels could drop to some valley floors Friday \nnight into Saturday morning. For now, however, the specifics remain \nuncertain.",
